"Trademark search (https://tmsearch.uspto.gov/?page=help)\nciteturn117search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; For example, to search for all live marks that contain the exact phrase \"happy birthday,\" you can search `CM:\"happy birthday\" AND LD:true`. ...   * `CM:\"this is my phrase\" AND LD:true` ... To find phrases in most fields, enter a regular expression for each word, then combine them. ... If the trademark you’re searching for is comprised of more than one term, be sure your regular expression search includes the most common spelling of at least one of the terms.\n\nYou can only search one word at a time with the options above.\n\n#### Complex searches\n\nYou can use the `Field tag and Search builder` option to do more complex searches.\n\nThis offers a few advantages:\n\n  * You can search any field of the database. For a complete list of fields, such as words in mark (CM) or goods and services (GS), see the `Advanced` tab.\n  * You can construct complex searches with multiple parameters. For example, to search for all live marks that contain the word \"dog,\" you can search `CM:dog AND LD:true`.\n  * You can search for an exact phrase. For example, to search for all live marks that contain the exact phrase \"happy birthday,\" you can search `CM:\"happy birthday\" AND LD:true`.\n\nField tag searches must follow a specific syntax, or search language. However, you can use the search builder to help you create complex searches as you learn the search syntax.\n\nSee the `Advanced` tab for instructions on complex searches, including how to use the search builder.\n\n### Viewing your search results\n\nYour search results show the basic details for each mark that matches your search. Select the image or wordmark to view additional details. You can still access the old TSDR view of your search results if you’re logged into your USPTO.gov account. Simply select the “Legacy view” option from the Print/save drop-down menu. A thick left-side border will appear on the tile of the individual search result to show that you've viewed it.\n\n\nDesign code\n\n`DC:030111`, or `DC:03.01.11` (see the design search code manual to find a design search code)\n\nSee the search fields section below for more information and a list of all fields.\n\nCommon search strings\n\nWe used placeholder text and numbers in the examples below. When you’re creating a search string, type your search term where you see `word`> or `this is my phrase`, and replace the example class numbers (`000`>) with the coordinated class or international class number that applies to your good or service.\n\n  * `CM:word AND LD:true`\nSearch for all live trademarks containing a particular word\n  * `CM:word AND LD:true AND CC:000`\nSearch for all live trademarks containing a particular word in a coordinated class\n  * `CM:word AND LD:true AND IC:(000 035 042 a b 200)`\nSearch for all live trademarks containing a particular word in a particular international class and the classes 35, 42, A, B, and 200\n  * `CM:\"this is my phrase\" AND LD:true`\nSearch for all live trademarks containing a particular phrase\n## Logical operators and wildcards\n\n#### Logical operators\n\nCombine multiple search terms or statements using logical operators. Logical operators must be in all capital letters.\n\n##### Common logical operators\n\n  * Use `AND` if results must satisfy both search terms.\nFor example, `AT:\"Jane Doe\" AND LD:true` retrieves records where Jane Doe is the attorney of record and the mark is live.\n  * Use `OR` if results must satisfy at least one search term.\nFor example, `ON:\"John Smith\" OR ON:\"John A. Smith\"` retrieves records where the owner is either John Smith or John A. Smith. Spaces are treated as `OR`, for example, `CM:(rain OR shine)` retrieves the same results as `CM:(rain shine)`.\n  * Use `AND NOT` if results must satisfy the first search term but not the second.\nFor example, `CM:*USPTO* AND NOT DC:*` retrieves marks where the literal element of the mark includes USPTO and there are no design search codes.\n\n##### Advanced logical operators\n\n  * Use `[ TO ]` to designate a range.\nFor example, `RD:[2021-01-01 TO 2021-12-31]` retrieves all marks with a registration date in 2021.\n  * Use `( )` to group sub-queries that happen first.\nFor example, `(CM:rain OR CM:shine) AND CM:thunder` retrieves all marks where the literal element includes either rain and thunder, or shine and thunder.\n  * Use `\" \"` if results must match the search term exactly.\nFor example, `CM:\"rain shine\"` retrieves marks where the literal element includes rain shine.\n  * Use `~` for proximity searching with quoted search terms.\nAllows words in quotes to appear with other words in-between or in a different order. Counts number words that are inserted, deleted, substituted, or transposed (called \"positions\"). For example, `CM:\"rain shine\"~5` retrieves:\n    * `rain shine` (no insertions)\n    * `shine rain` (one transposition)\n    * `rain and it might shine` (three insertions) and\n    * `shine on rain` (one transposition and one insertion)\nbut doesn’t retrieve:\n    * `Shine your car to protect from rain` (one transposition and five insertions)\n\nYou can also use logical operators to combine multiple search lines. For example, `1 AND 2` retrieves marks that meet the criteria for both your first and second searches. ## [Button: Numbers ]\n\nNumber  | String to enter  | Example\n--- | --- | ---\nNumbers up to 9  | `[0-9]`  | `/pro[0-9]/` matches pro1\nEven digits  | `[02468]`  | `/pro[02468]/` matches pro2\nOdd digits  | `[13579]`  | `/pro[13579]/` matches pro3\nLetters and numbers up to 9  | `[a-z0-9]`  | `/pro[a-z0-9]/` matches prop, pro1\n\n### Reserved characters\n\nThe following characters are reserved, which means you must search for them differently because they’re used in regular expression syntax: `. ? + * | { } [ ] ( ) \" \\ / # @ & < > ~ - : ;`\n\nHowever, there are two ways to search for reserved characters themselves.\n\n  * Enter a backslash before the reserved character. For example, `CM:/tmarkey\\+/` returns the mark TMarkey+.\n  * Enter the reserved character inside brackets. For example, `CM:/tmarkey[+]/` returns the mark TMarkey+.\n\nTo find a mark comprised only of a reserved character, or to find any mark that uses one of the reserved characters, use the full mark field.\n\n  * Either `FM:/\\?/` or `FM:/[?]/` retrieves the mark ?.\n  * Either `FM:/.*\\+.*/` or `FM:/.*[+].*/` retrieves any mark that includes a plus sign.\n\n### Phrases and hyphenated terms\n\nGenerally, regular expressions don’t match spaces.\n\n  * `CM:/.*ful.*scal.*/`\nRetrieves FULL-SCALE and FULLSCALE, but not FULL SCALE\n\nTo find phrases in most fields, enter a regular expression for each word, then combine them.\n\n  * `CM:/.*ful.*/ AND CM:/.*scal.*/`\nRetrieves FULL-SCALE, FULLSCALE, and FULL SCALE\n\nHyphens are a reserved character. To specifically search for a hyphenated phrase, use a backslash or brackets.\n\n  * `CM:/.*ful.[-]scal.*/`\nReturns FULL-SCALE\n\nThe full mark field is an exception regarding spaces. Spaces are treated as characters in this field.\n\n  * `FM:/full.*scale/`\nRetrieves FULL-SCALE, FULLSCALE and FULL SCALE\n\n### Exact matches\n\nWhen searching for conflicting marks, ensure your search will return marks that match yours exactly. Although regular expression searching can help you find marks with alternate spellings, you want to make sure your search returns results for the common spelling of your mark.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTransitioning from TESS to the new search system (https://www.uspto.gov/sites/default/files/documents/TM-Transitioning-from-TESS-handout.pdf)\nciteturn117search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.4 years ago; Crawled: 2.2 years ago; For each mark in our database, data is stored in distinct fields, and you can use field tags to search the various fields individually. ... <td>CM:</td> .